 | `D-47` | 2026-09-17 | **Image size is measured as content size, with the containerd image store, and CI enables it explicitly.** `docker image inspect .Size` is the measure named by `W-49` §9 item 11; CI configures the containerd image store before building so that measure means the same thing everywhere, and the size step refuses to run if that store is not active | `.Size` reports uncompressed layer diffs under the classic overlay2 driver and content size — the compressed blobs actually pulled — under the containerd image store. The same backend image measured **291 MB** one way and **154 MB** the other, so the 200 MB threshold passed on a developer's laptop and failed on CI while nothing about the image had changed. Compressed size is what a Container Apps pull actually costs, and the approved 2026-09-15 baseline was taken that way. The alternative, raising the threshold to fit whatever the runner happened to report, would have made the number mean nothing. The refusal matters more than the number: a threshold is only meaningful if the measure behind it is the one that was approved | `04` §5, `W-49`, `W-59` |
 | `D-48` | 2026-09-15 | **Two jars in one backend image, selected by `INFINEVO_ROLE`** — not one fat jar toggled by a Spring profile. Supersedes the `Spring profile` row of `04` §3 | `code/backend/app` and `code/backend/worker` are separate Maven packaging modules that each produce a fat jar over the identical domain libraries. Both are copied into the same image and `backend-entrypoint.sh` runs the one named by `INFINEVO_ROLE`, defaulting to `app` and exiting non-zero on anything else. Consequence, and the reason it is worth recording: schedulers and batch work are **physically** isolated to `worker.jar`, so an `app` replica cannot run a scheduled job even by misconfiguration. `W-52` depends on this shape | `04` §1 §3, `D-02`, `W-49`, `W-52` |
 | `D-49` | 2026-09-15 | **The frontend runs unprivileged nginx on port 8080**, `nginxinc/nginx-unprivileged:alpine` as uid 101, rather than standard `nginx:alpine` on port 80 | Binding port 80 requires root. Moving to 8080 lets the whole container process run unprivileged, which is what makes "every container runs non-root" true without exception rather than true for two of three. `/usr/share/nginx/html` is chowned to 101 so the entrypoint can write `env.js` without privilege | `04` §1 §4, `W-49`, `W-59` |
-| `D-44` | 2026-09-13 | **RabbitMQ stands in for Azure Service Bus in the local stack.** Production remains Service Bus (`D-10`) — this is a laptop substitute only | No faithful local emulator exists with acceptable licence terms. `W-52` builds a queue abstraction, so local and production differ by one adapter, which is the point of having the abstraction. The alternative, a Postgres-backed queue, tests nothing about real queue behaviour | `04` §6, `W-02` |
+| ~~`D-44`~~ | 2026-09-13 | ~~**RabbitMQ stands in for Azure Service Bus in the local stack.** Production remains Service Bus (`D-10`) — this is a laptop substitute only~~ — **superseded by `D-50`, 2026-09-19.** Production is Storage Queue, which Azurite emulates faithfully, so the local stand-in is Azurite rather than RabbitMQ | No faithful local emulator exists with acceptable licence terms. `W-52` builds a queue abstraction, so local and production differ by one adapter, which is the point of having the abstraction. The alternative, a Postgres-backed queue, tests nothing about real queue behaviour | `04` §6, `W-02` |
+| `D-50` | 2026-09-19 | **Azure Storage Queue replaces Azure Service Bus for the job queue.** Supersedes `D-44` | A private endpoint on Service Bus is **Premium-tier only** — Microsoft: *"This feature is supported with the premium tier"* — at roughly ten times Standard, which `D-19` scale (10 tenants x 100 employees) does not justify. Storage Queue takes a private endpoint on the Standard tier, so the queue satisfies `05` §4 — *"Only Front Door is public. Nothing else has a public endpoint"* — at no extra cost, in the storage account that already exists for documents. The alternative considered and rejected was leaving Service Bus on a public endpoint gated by Entra ID RBAC alone. **Accepted losses:** no topics or subscriptions, no message sessions and therefore no guaranteed ordering, no scheduled messages, and 64 KB per message rather than 256 KB. Founder judged the ordering loss unrealistic at this scale; the consequence is that **`W-52` handles idempotency in code rather than relying on broker ordering** | `04` §6, `05` §3, `D-10`, `D-44`, `W-51`, `W-52` |
 
